TZ(5)                          P32W Unix                           TZ(5)

NAME
       tz - time zone: the TZ variable and /etc/TZ

SYNOPSIS
       TZ=std offset[dst[offset][,start[/time],end[/time]]]

DESCRIPTION
       The kernel and the clock keep UTC; the files' times are UTC.
       The C library's localtime and mktime (see time(3)) convert
       to and from local time by a POSIX time-zone rule, taken from
       the environment variable TZ or, when TZ is unset or empty,
       from the first line of /etc/TZ, which sets the zone for
       every user. With neither, local time is UTC. date(1), ls(1),
       who(1), cron(8) and the other programs that show or take
       times all use it.

       The rule's parts, with no blanks between them:

       std, dst
               the names of standard and summer (daylight saving)
               time: letters only, at most 15, or any characters
               inside < and >. Without dst there is no summer time.

       offset  how far the zone is west of Greenwich, as
               [+|-]hh[:mm[:ss]]: positive west (behind UTC),
               negative east. std's offset defaults to 0; dst's to
               one hour less than std's.

       start, end
               when summer time begins and ends, as Mm.w.d: day d
               (0 = Sunday .. 6) of week w (1 .. 4, or 5 for the
               last) of month m (1 .. 12). The time after / is the
               local time of the change, [+|-]hh[:mm[:ss]], by
               default 02:00. The start is given in standard time,
               the end in summer time. If end comes before start
               in the year (the southern hemisphere), summer time
               spans the new year.

       With dst and no rule, the US rule applies: from the second
       Sunday in March to the first Sunday in November, at 02:00.
       A leading : is skipped.

       The zone is read once in each process, at the first call of
       localtime or mktime; a program that changes TZ afterwards
       must call tzset. The names go to tzname[0] and tzname[1].

EXAMPLES
       TZ=PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0          US Pacific
       TZ=EST5EDT                         US Eastern (the US rule)
       TZ=CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3      central Europe
       TZ=AEST-10AEDT,M10.1.0,M4.1.0/3    Sydney
       TZ=JST-9                           Japan, no summer time
       TZ=IST-5:30                        India

       To set the zone for everyone, as root:

              echo PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 > /etc/TZ

FILES
       /etc/TZ        the default rule (not shipped: absent, the
                      default is UTC)

ENVIRONMENT
       TZ             the rule; overrides /etc/TZ

SEE ALSO
       date(1), time(3), sh(1), cron(8), crontab(5)

BUGS
       There is no time-zone database: names such as
       America/New_York are not understood (the letters before the
       / are taken as a zone name with offset 0). The rule forms Jn
       and n (days of the year) are not supported; the US rule is
       used instead. A malformed rule is not reported. Only the
       first 63 characters of /etc/TZ are read.
